A wrapper leaves room for its caller to supply content. Mark that place with
`$slot`, then put the wrapper in the head of a new template.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    card #= [$html:<article>[$slot]</article>]
    notice = [card:All tests passed.]
]

The default slot receives the body. Here `notice` contains
`<article>All tests passed.</article>`.

Give a slot a name when the caller has more than one kind of content to
supply. Use `$in` to send content to that name.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    panel #= [$html:<h2>[$slot title = "Draft"]</h2><section>[$slot]</section>]
    report = [panel:[$in title:Build]All tests passed.]
]

`Build` appears in the heading and the sentence appears in the section.
If you omit the title insert, the heading uses `Draft`. An explicitly empty
insert leaves the heading empty. A numbered slot such as `$slot 1` receives
a loose contribution by position. See @../template-directives/#slot-directives
(Slots and inserts) for a positional example and presence guards.
